<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsugaru Tamenobu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1550">1550</a>–<a href="/years/1608">1608</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ōura Tamenobu · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-clan">Tsugaru clan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsugaru Tamenobu, born Ōura Tamenobu, was a Japanese warlord who seized the Tsugaru region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> from his overlords, the Nanbu, in the 1570s and 1580s. Recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> in 1590, he took the name Tsugaru and sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>. He was the founder of the Hirosaki domain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsugaru-tamenobu.jpg" alt="Tsugaru Tamenobu" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 不明。 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tugaru_Tamenobu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tamenobu was born on 18 January 1550 and was the adopted son and heir of Ōura Tamenori, a retainer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanbu-clan">Nanbu clan</a>, whose seat was Sannohe Castle. He succeeded his father in 1567 or 1568 as castellan of Ōura Castle, in what is now part of the city of Hirosaki. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-clan">Tsugaru clan</a> records traced the family to the noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a>, while the records of their rivals, the Nanbu, held that he came from a minor branch of the Nanbu and had been driven out after discord with his elder brother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Ōura were hereditary deputies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanbu-clan">Nanbu clan</a>'s local magistrate, Ishikawa Takanobu. In 1571 Tamenobu attacked and killed Ishikawa and went on to take the Nanbu castles of the region one after another, among them Ishikawa, Daikoji and Aburakawa. After the death of Nanbu Harumasa in 1582 the Nanbu split into factions, and Tamenobu declared the territories he held independent. Proclaimed a traitor, he sought the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, but his attempts to travel south in 1586, 1587 and 1588 were blocked by hostile forces.</p><p>In 1589 he sent horses and falcons for Hideyoshi through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> and asked to be recognised as daimyō of the three Tsugaru districts of Hiraga, Hanawa and Inaka, assessed at 45,000 koku. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanbu-nobunao">Nanbu Nobunao</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-toshiie">Maeda Toshiie</a>, demanded his punishment as a rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, but Tamenobu had the support of Ishida Mitsunari, Hashiba Hidetsugu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobukatsu">Oda Nobukatsu</a> and had pledged fealty earlier than Nobunao. He joined the suppression of the Kunohe Rebellion in 1591 and moved his seat to Horikoshi Castle in 1597.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His reward for Sekigahara was only 2000 koku in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kozuke-province">Kozuke Province</a>, which brought the domain to 47,000 koku. Afterwards he sheltered the orphaned children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> and married his son Nobuhira to Mitsunari's daughter. A rising during his absence briefly held Horikoshi Castle, and he then began a new castle on a large scale at Takaoka, between the Iwaki and Tabuchi rivers, which became Hirosaki Castle.</p><p>He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> at the age of 58, having set out to visit his eldest son, who had fallen ill there and died before he arrived. He was buried at Kakushū-ji, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-clan">Tsugaru clan</a> temple in Hirosaki. As his second son, Nobukata, had died in 1597, the succession went to Nobuhira, which many retainers opposed in favour of the eldest son's son, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> intervened and confirmed Nobuhira.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tsugaru Tamenobu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Oura Tamenobu</b>, a vassal of the Nanbu, seized the Tsugaru region in the 1570s and 1580s and won recognition directly from Toyotomi Hideyoshi in 1590, taking the name Tsugaru.</blockquote><a href="/state/hirosaki-domain">Hirosaki Domain</a></li></ul></section>
